        <body>
  <h1>Student Rights & Discipline in Syracuse City SD: A Parent's Guide</h1>

  <p>The Syracuse City School District is committed to providing a safe and supportive learning environment for all students. However, navigating disciplinary actions, especially for students with disabilities, can be challenging. This guide provides essential information about your rights and responsibilities, focusing on Manifestation Determination Reviews (MDRs), suspensions, and due process rights within the Syracuse City SD context.</p>

  <h2>Understanding Key Terms</h2>

  Before diving into the specifics, let's define some key terms:

  <ul>
    <li><b>IEP (Individualized Education Program):</b> A legally binding document outlining a student's special education needs and the supports and services they will receive.  Governed by the Individuals with Disabilities Education Act (IDEA).</li>
    <li><b>FBA (Functional Behavioral Assessment):</b> A process used to understand the purpose or function of a student's behavior. It helps identify triggers, antecedents, and consequences that maintain the behavior.</li>
    <li><b>BIP (Behavior Intervention Plan):</b> A plan developed based on the FBA that outlines strategies and interventions to address a student's challenging behavior. Must be individualized to the student's needs.  See NYS Part 200.22.</li>
    <li><b>Suspension:</b> Temporary removal of a student from school, either in-school or out-of-school.</li>
    <li><b>Change in Placement:</b> A disciplinary action that results in a student being removed from their current educational setting for more than 10 consecutive school days or a series of removals that constitute a pattern.</li>
    <li><b>Manifestation Determination:</b> A process to determine if a student's misconduct is related to their disability.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Manifestation Determination Review (MDR): A Critical Safeguard</h2>

  <p>The Manifestation Determination Review (MDR) is a critical protection for students with IEPs facing disciplinary action that could result in a suspension exceeding 10 school days or a change in placement. This review is mandated by the Individuals with Disabilities Education Act (IDEA) and implemented under New York State regulations (specifically, Part 201 of the Regulations of the Commissioner of Education).</p>

  <p><b>The Purpose of the MDR:</b></p>

  <p>The MDR's primary goal is to determine whether the student's behavior leading to the disciplinary action was:</p>

  <ol>
    <li><b>Caused by or directly and substantially related to the student's disability; OR</b></li>
    <li><b>The direct result of the school district's failure to implement the student's IEP.</b></li>
  </ol>

  <p>If either of these conditions is met, the behavior is considered a "manifestation" of the student's disability.</p>

  <p><b>Who Participates in the MDR?</b></p>

  <p>The MDR team typically includes:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>The student's parents or guardians</li>
    <li>Relevant members of the IEP team, including special education teachers, general education teachers, school psychologists, and administrators.</li>
    <li>The student (when appropriate – age and ability should be considered).</li>
  </ul>

  <p><b>The MDR Process: A Step-by-Step Guide for Parents</b></p>

  <ol>
    <li><b>Notification:</b> You must receive timely notification from the school district about the disciplinary action and the scheduled MDR meeting. Ensure the notification is clear about the purpose of the meeting and your rights.</li>
    <li><b>Record Review:</b> The MDR team will review the student's file, including the IEP, any relevant evaluations, and teacher observations.  Critically, the team should review the *implementation* of the IEP, not just the document itself.  Were the accommodations and modifications actually provided?  Was data collected on their effectiveness?</li>
    <li><b>Information Gathering:</b> The team will consider all relevant information, including input from parents, teachers, and the student. Share your perspective on the student's behavior, potential triggers, and the effectiveness of current interventions. Bring any documentation you feel is relevant.</li>
    <li><b>Determination:</b> The team will make a determination based on the information reviewed. This determination must be documented in writing.</li>
    <li><b>Outcomes:</b>
      <ul>
        <li><b>If the behavior IS a manifestation:</b> The IEP team must conduct a Functional Behavioral Assessment (FBA), unless one has already been conducted.  Based on the FBA, the team must develop or revise a Behavior Intervention Plan (BIP) to address the behavior.  The student must be returned to their original placement, unless the parents and the district agree to a change of placement as part of the BIP.</li>
        <li><b>If the behavior is NOT a manifestation:</b> The student may be subject to the same disciplinary procedures as students without disabilities. However, the school district must still provide Free Appropriate Public Education (FAPE), ensuring the student continues to receive services outlined in their IEP. This may involve providing services in an alternative setting.</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
  </ol>

  <p><b>Parent Advocacy Tips for the MDR:</b></p>

  <ul>
    <li><b>Prepare Thoroughly:</b> Review your child's IEP, behavioral data, and any relevant reports.  Document instances where the IEP was not implemented correctly.</li>
    <li><b>Document Everything:</b> Keep records of all communication with the school, including emails, phone calls, and meeting notes.</li>
    <li><b>Bring Support:</b> You have the right to bring an advocate or attorney to the MDR meeting.  This can be extremely helpful.</li>
    <li><b>Be Assertive:</b> Clearly and respectfully communicate your concerns and perspectives. Don't be afraid to ask questions and challenge assumptions.</li>
    <li><b>Focus on the IEP:</b> Emphasize the importance of implementing the IEP and addressing any unmet needs.</li>
    <li><b>Request an Independent Educational Evaluation (IEE):</b> If you disagree with the school district's evaluation, you have the right to request an IEE at public expense (under certain conditions).</li>
  </ul>

  <p><b>Common District Pitfalls to Watch Out For:</b></p>

  <ul>
    <li><b>Failure to properly implement the IEP:</b> This is a frequent issue. Ensure that all accommodations and modifications are being provided consistently.</li>
    <li><b>Inadequate FBA or BIP:</b> The FBA must be comprehensive and the BIP must be individualized and evidence-based.</li>
    <li><b>Predetermined Outcomes:</b> The MDR must be a genuine review, not a pre-determined decision.</li>
    <li><b>Lack of Parental Involvement:</b> Your input is critical to the MDR process. Ensure your voice is heard.</li>
    <li><b>Insufficient Documentation:</b> The district must maintain accurate and complete records.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Navigating Suspensions in Syracuse City SD</h2>

  <p>Understanding the suspension process in Syracuse City SD is crucial for protecting your child's rights. New York State Education Law and Syracuse City SD's own policies govern student suspensions.</p>

  <p><b>Types of Suspensions:</b></p>

  <ul>
    <li><b>Short-term Suspension (5 days or less):</b> Requires oral or written notification to the parent and an opportunity for an informal conference.</li>
    <li><b>Long-term Suspension (more than 5 days):</b> Requires written notice of the charges, the right to a hearing, and the right to be represented by an attorney. (See NYS Education Law § 3214)</li>
  </ul>

  <p><b>Your Rights During a Suspension:</b></p>

  <ul>
    <li><b>Right to Notification:</b> You have the right to be notified of the reasons for the suspension and the length of the suspension. The notice must be clear and specific.</li>
    <li><b>Right to a Hearing:</b> For suspensions exceeding 5 days, you have the right to a formal hearing where you can present evidence and question witnesses.</li>
    <li><b>Right to Representation:</b> You have the right to be represented by an attorney at the hearing.</li>
    <li><b>Right to Access Records:</b> You have the right to access your child's school records related to the suspension.</li>
    <li><b>Right to Appeal:</b> You have the right to appeal the suspension decision.</li>
  </ul>

  <p><b>Steps to Take If Your Child Is Suspended:</b></p>

  <ol>
    <li><b>Request a Written Explanation:</b> Immediately request a written explanation of the charges leading to the suspension. This will help you understand the basis for the disciplinary action.</li>
    <li><b>Attend All Meetings and Hearings:</b> Actively participate in all scheduled meetings and hearings. Ask questions, present your perspective, and provide any relevant information.</li>
    <li><b>Document Everything:</b> Keep detailed notes of all conversations, meetings, and correspondence. Obtain copies of all relevant documents.</li>
    <li><b>Seek Legal Advice:</b> If you believe the suspension is unwarranted or violates your child's rights, consult with an attorney specializing in special education law.</li>
    <li><b>Explore Alternative Solutions:</b> Work collaboratively with the school to explore alternative solutions to address the behavioral concerns. This may include counseling, behavior support, or modifications to the IEP.</li>
  </ol>

  <p><b>Challenging a Suspension:</b></p>

  <p>If you believe your child's suspension was unfair or violated their rights, you have several options:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><b>Appeal to the Superintendent:</b> You can appeal the principal's decision to the Superintendent of the Syracuse City School District.</li>
    <li><b>Appeal to the Board of Education:</b> If the Superintendent upholds the suspension, you can appeal to the Board of Education.</li>
    <li><b>File a Complaint with the New York State Education Department (NYSED):</b> You can file a complaint with NYSED if you believe the school district violated state or federal law.</li>
    <li><b>Initiate Due Process:</b> If the suspension constitutes a change in placement (more than 10 consecutive days or a pattern of removals), you have the right to initiate due process proceedings under IDEA.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Due Process Rights Under IDEA</h2>

  <p>The Individuals with Disabilities Education Act (IDEA) provides significant due process rights to parents of students with disabilities. These rights protect your ability to participate in decisions about your child's education and to challenge decisions you disagree with.</p>

  <p><b>Key Due Process Rights:</b></p>

  <ul>
    <li><b>Right to Prior Written Notice:</b> The school district must provide you with written notice before taking any action that affects your child's education, including evaluations, placement changes, and disciplinary actions. The notice must explain the proposed action, the reasons for the action, and your rights.</li>
    <li><b>Right to Participate in Meetings:</b> You have the right to participate in all meetings related to your child's IEP, including IEP meetings, manifestation determination reviews, and disciplinary hearings.</li>
    <li><b>Right to Examine Records:</b> You have the right to access and examine all of your child's education records.</li>
    <li><b>Right to an Independent Educational Evaluation (IEE):</b> If you disagree with the school district's evaluation of your child, you have the right to request an IEE at public expense (under certain conditions).</li>
    <li><b>Right to Mediation:</b> You have the right to participate in mediation to resolve disputes with the school district.</li>
    <li><b>Right to a Due Process Hearing:</b> If you are unable to resolve your dispute through mediation, you have the right to request a due process hearing before an impartial hearing officer.</li>
  </ul>

  <p><b>Initiating Due Process:</b></p>

  <p>To initiate due process, you must file a written complaint with the New York State Education Department (NYSED). The complaint must include specific information, such as the nature of the dispute, the facts related to the dispute, and the relief you are seeking.</p>

  <p><b>Resources for Parents in Syracuse City SD:</b></p>
  <ul>
   <li><a href="https://www.syracusecityschools.com/" target="_blank">Syracuse City School District Website</a></li>
   <li><a href="https://www.nysid.nysed.gov/" target="_blank">New York State Education Department</a></li>
   <li><a href="https://www.disabilityrightsnys.org/" target="_blank">Disability Rights New York</a></li>
   <li>Your child's IEP team members (teachers, counselors, etc.)</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ)</h2>

  <dl>
    <dt><b>Q: What is the difference between a suspension and expulsion?</b></dt>
    <dd>A: A suspension is a temporary removal from school, while an expulsion is a permanent removal. Expulsion is rare in New York State, especially for students with disabilities.</dd>

    <dt><b>Q: Can my child be suspended for behavior related to their disability?</b></dt>
    <dd>A: It depends. If the behavior is determined to be a manifestation of their disability, they cannot be suspended for more than 10 days unless the IEP team agrees to a change of placement.</dd>

    <dt><b>Q: What if my child doesn't have a formal IEP but I suspect they have a disability?</b></dt>
    <dd>A: You have the right to request an evaluation to determine if your child is eligible for special education services. Put your request in writing to the school principal and the district's special education director. The school has a specific timeframe in which they must respond to your request and conduct the evaluation. If the school refuses to evaluate, you can still pursue an IEE at public expense.</dd>

    <dt><b>Q: The school is refusing to provide my child's IEP accommodations. What can I do?</b></dt>
    <dd>A: Document the instances where accommodations are not being provided. Communicate your concerns in writing to the school principal, special education teacher, and case manager. Request an IEP meeting to address the issue. If the problem persists, consider filing a complaint with NYSED or initiating due process.</dd>

     <dt><b>Q: What is an impartial hearing officer?</b></dt>
    <dd>A:  An impartial hearing officer (IHO) is an attorney who has experience in special education law.  They are appointed by NYSED to conduct due process hearings and make legally binding decisions on disputes between parents and school districts.</dd>
  </dl>

</body>
